Šarić refers to an insight from the sociology of risk, an area from which he can say something concrete. The general perception of fears and risks that society takes on, whether it is a climate cataclysm or the catastrophe of a nuclear reactor, is **to a good extent culturally conditioned**. It is shaped by content that a person receives on social networks, films, articles, radio, and in the end podcasts as well. What is presented as absolutely terrifying and potentially destructive turns in the psyche and subconscious of the individual.

When nuclear energy becomes the subject of the day, the individual feels a fear that is not rational and calculated, but a fear for which culture has "sold him a story" that he must be afraid of it. Šarić illustrates this with a personal anecdote. In childhood he watched a documentary about black holes with a visual of a black hole passing through the Earth and devouring it. He thought that this would probably happen in his lifetime. It was even said that an experiment at CERN would create a small black hole that would suck in the Earth, but this did not happen. When he later researched it, he saw that such possibilities are not real, and at that time the perception of fear was hyperinflated. The same holds for many people and problems that they take seriously only because they are constantly mentioned in the public space.

For more banal fears he cites several examples:

- statistically it is much more dangerous to ride in a personal car, for example in Zagreb, than to fly by airplane, and yet people are afraid of airplanes because to them it is "magic";
- spiders and snakes cause fear, although, as Marić adds, snakes are incomparably more afraid of us than we are of them;
- the mosquito is responsible for hundreds of thousands of deaths annually around the world because it transmits diseases, and we perceive it only as a nuisance, although these diseases are not transmitted here.
